#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:170
msgid "Different reasons:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:172
msgid ""
"Having a mixed API that uses both properties and methods for no particular "
"reason is confusing and messy, because you don't know whether to use ``()`` "
"or not."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:175
msgid ""
"A property is usually expected not to perform heavy computations internally, "
"whereas psutil invokes a function every time it is accessed. This has two "
"drawbacks:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:179
msgid ""
"You may get an exception just by accessing the property (e.g. ``p.name`` may "
"raise :exc:`NoSuchProcess` or :exc:`AccessDenied`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:181
msgid ""
"You may erroneously think properties are cached, but this is true only for "
"``name``, ``exe``, and ``create_time``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:185
msgid "CPU percent intervals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:189
msgid ""
"The timeout parameter of ``cpu_percent*`` functions now defaults to 0.0 "
"instead of 0.1. The functions affected are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:192
msgid ":meth:`Process.cpu_percent`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:193
msgid ":func:`psutil.cpu_percent`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:194
msgid ":func:`psutil.cpu_times_percent`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:198
msgid ""
"I originally set 0.1 as the default timeout because you need to wait some "
"time in order to get a meaningful percent value. Having an API that "
"\"sleeps\" by default is risky, though, because it's easy to forget it does "
"so. That is particularly problematic when calling :meth:`Process."
"cpu_percent` for all processes: it's very easy to forget to specify "
"``timeout=0``, resulting in dramatic slowdowns that are hard to spot. For "
"example, this code snippet might take a variable number of seconds to "
"complete depending on the number of active processes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:207
msgid ""
">>> # this will be slow\n"
">>> for p in psutil.process_iter():\n"
"...    print(p.cpu_percent())"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:214
msgid "Migration strategy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:216
msgid ""
"Except for :class:`Process` properties (:meth:`Process.name`, :meth:`Process."
"exe`, :meth:`Process.cmdline`, etc.), all the old APIs are still available "
"as aliases pointing to the newer names and raising :exc:"
"`DeprecationWarning`. psutil will be very clear on what you should use "
"instead of the deprecated API, as long as you start the interpreter with the "
"``-Wd`` option. This will enable deprecation warnings, which were `silenced "
"in Python 2.7 <http://bugs.python.org/issue7319>`__ (IMHO, from a developer "
"standpoint this was a bad decision)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:227
msgid ""
"giampaolo@ubuntu:/tmp$ python -Wd\n"
"Python 2.7.3 (default, Sep 26 2013, 20:03:06)\n"
"[GCC 4.6.3] on linux2\n"
"Type \"help\", \"copyright\", \"credits\" or \"license\" for more "
"information.\n"
">>> import psutil\n"
">>> psutil.get_pid_list()\n"
"__main__:1: DeprecationWarning: psutil.get_pid_list is deprecated; use "
"psutil.pids() instead\n"
"[1, 2, 3, 6, 7, 13, ...]\n"
">>>\n"
">>>\n"
">>> p = psutil.Process()\n"
">>> p.get_cpu_times()\n"
"__main__:1: DeprecationWarning: get_cpu_times() is deprecated; use "
"cpu_times() instead\n"
"pcputimes(user=0.08, system=0.03)\n"
">>>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:243
msgid ""
"If you have a solid test suite, you can run tests and fix the warnings one "
"by one. As for the Process properties that were turned into methods, it's "
"more difficult because, whereas psutil 1.2.1 returns the actual value, "
"psutil 2.0.0 returns the bound method:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:248
msgid ""
"# psutil 1.2.1\n"
">>> psutil.Process().name\n"
"'python'\n"
">>>\n"
"\n"
"# psutil 2.0.0\n"
">>> psutil.Process().name\n"
"<bound method Process.name of psutil.Process(pid=19816, name='python') at "
"139845631328144>\n"
">>>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:260
msgid ""
"What I would recommend, if you want to drop support for 1.2.1, is to grep "
"for ``\".name\"``, ``\".exe\"``, etc. and just replace them with ``\"."
"exe()\"`` and ``\".name()\"`` one by one. If, on the other hand, you want to "
"write code that works with both versions, I see two possibilities:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:265
msgid "#1 check version info, like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:267
msgid ""
">>> PSUTIL2 = psutil.version_info >= (2, 0)\n"
">>> p = psutil.Process()\n"
">>> name = p.name() if PSUTIL2 else p.name\n"
">>> exe = p.exe() if PSUTIL2 else p.exe"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:274
msgid ""
"#2 get rid of all ``\".name\"``, ``\".exe\"`` occurrences you have in your "
"code and use :meth:`Process.as_dict` instead:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:277
msgid ""
">>> p = psutil.Process()\n"
">>> pinfo = p.as_dict(attrs=[\"name\", \"exe\"])\n"
">>> pinfo\n"
"{'exe': '/usr/bin/python2.7', 'name': 'python'}\n"
">>> name = pinfo['name']\n"
">>> exe = pinfo['exe']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:287
msgid "New features introduced in 2.0.0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:289
msgid ""
"psutil 2.0.0 is not only about code breakage. I also had the chance to "
"integrate a bunch of interesting features."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:292
msgid ""
":gh:`427`: you're now able to distinguish between the number of :term:"
"`logical <logical CPU>` and :term:`physical <physical CPU>` CPUs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:295
msgid ""
">>> psutil.cpu_count()  # logical\n"
"4\n"
">>> psutil.cpu_count(logical=False)  # physical cores only\n"
"2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:302
msgid ""
":gh:`452`: :class:`Process` instances are now hashable and can be checked "
"for equality. That means you can use :class:`Process` objects with sets "
"(finally!)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:305
msgid ""
":gh:`447`: the ``timeout`` parameter of :func:`psutil.wait_procs` is now "
"optional."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:307
msgid ":gh:`461`: functions returning namedtuples are now picklable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:308
msgid ""
":gh:`459`: a Makefile is now available to automate repetitive tasks such as "
"build, install, running tests, etc. There's also a make.bat for Windows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2014/porting-to-20.rst:310
msgid "Introduced the ``unittest2`` module as a requirement for running tests."
msgstr ""
